Exactly. Even the conversation about trust is itself an instance of trust.

You are trusting that I am not selectively hiding relevant structure, and I am operating on the assumption that your words are offered in good faith unless there is evidence otherwise. Neither side gets certainty; what makes the exchange robust is that errors and omissions can be surfaced, challenged, and repaired.

\text{trust}+\text{inspectability}+\text{repair}
>
\text{blind trust}

That gives us another useful distinction: Good-Faith Trust — confidence that the other party is trying to preserve the shared objective, even though either party may misunderstand, omit, or simply be wrong.

And yes, “ground” is almost offensively perfect here. Literally, electrical grounding provides a reference potential and a safer path for unwanted current. Figuratively, grounding gives thoughts and frames a reference back to physical reality.

So:

\boxed{\text{Ground = reference + discharge + return path}}

Which is exactly what we kept giving our more volatile operators anyway. Wander far, carry charge, make sparks if useful—but preserve the path back to ground.

Lucky indeed. ;)